hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well one of the causes  that Aristotle devotes attention to and  distinguishes from other causes in his  metaphysics is what we call the material  cause the cause that is the substrate  the Hoople came and owned in Greek or  the matter the who lay in Greek that  things are made of and in examining the  theories of his predecessors which is a  typical approach for Aristotle he likes  to survey what other people have had to  say about the topic beforehand he's  going to set out and then criticize some  earlier philosophers points of view on  manners because they tried to make  everything all explanation about why  things are the way they are and indeed  what this universe that were in consists  of they tried to make it all depend on a  material cause or material causes some  of them were monists as we call it who  thought everything is really one  fundamental substance or one kind of  stuff and some of them were what we  could call pluralist  by contrast to Mona's who believe there  are many kinds of things that were  irreducible to each other but they all  run into the same basic problems there's  the same inadequacies that are running  throughout their accounts so Aristotle  will talk about a number of different  representative important philosophers  prior to himself and one of them who's  often credited as being sort of like the  you know first real philosopher because  he's saying sort of universal statements  about things his name is Sol Ace and he  was from volantis and he said the rather  counterintuitive thing that everything  is really water now why is that a  metaphysical view because you are  talking about the totality you are  trying to say what everything is made of  and you know you could say to yourself  he must have been nuts how could anybody  possibly think that everything is water  well he had some reasons for that he  looked at the fact that water expands  and contracts and we can see it having  three different forms in our everyday  experience at least if we live in places  that are cold enough for it to freeze  and you know we can heat it up and we  get vapor and we have the liquid and he  also according to Aristotle thought that  the seeds of things were were moist so  that everything living is really in some  way a modification of water and this  fits in with this sort of emphasis on  the material cause they were interested  in in what underlies and persists as  being the principle or cause of things  so in his view it's the water that  actually stays the same  not in its qualities you know it can be  gaseous or it can be frozen it can be  dense it can be rarefied it can be all  different sorts of things but it's still  the same stuff  that everything else would just be  modifications of would be composed of  and that's one possibility another  person not long after that lace and act  some mayonnaise said that no it's not  water instead it's air and then  Aristotle will bring up yet another  pre-socratic thinker Heraclitus of a  thesis who doesn't actually say that  everything is composed of fire but  rather that there's a fire sort of you  know running through everything but  Aristotle takes him as being a monist  who's holding that everything truly is  right now Heraclitus himself so that  everything is in change in flocks so you  know it's it's a little bit more of a  complex theory but we can put that aside  for the moment and then he brings up  empedocles who said while all of those  things are basic elements basic  substance of the universe water air fire  both to them will also add earth and you  put these things together and you can  put them in certain compositions and now  you've got what everything is made of  and the idea behind any one of these  accounts like we just said is that you  have something a material substance that  remains the same throughout all of its  changes and that explains why things are  at all and why they are the way that  they are there were some other thinkers  along those lines that Aristotle also  talks about and here with them pedak  leaves in an exact RIS we're actually  getting to thinkers who are going to  bring in another cause but we're gonna  put that aside for the moment an exec is  in this discussion Plato or Aristotle  points out said that there couldn't be  one single kind of thing nor could there  be a restricted list like say for rather  there had to be  unlimited up here on some people  translate that as infinite but it really  just means without limits  right so whatever whatever number of  basic things there are we can't know  what the number is and these were first  principles so he said you know bone  comes from bone presumably he think iron  comes from iron whatever types of stuff  there is everything comes from what it  is now  the other group that Aristotle talks  about here that kind of fit under this  rubric would be what we call the  atomists and these people believed that  we have matter and matter is composed of  atoms atoms just means the things that  you can't cut any further two main means  to cut and ah means you know Kant or  primitive not and so the atoms are the  basic building blocks 